---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT, SILENTSILO, REPO } from "../links";
---
<Base title="Who makes this" description="whereti is made by Alex Stanciu through Software Hive S.R.L., the company that also makes SilentSilo." prose>
  <h1>Who makes this</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    whereti is made by Alex Stanciu, through Software Hive S.R.L., a small software company in Romania. The same company
    makes <a href={SILENTSILO}>SilentSilo</a>, an encrypted vault for files and passwords.
  </p>

  <h2>Why whereti</h2>
  <p>
    Finding a file on Windows should take the time it takes to type its name. The search built into Windows often does
    not find it at all, and the good tools that do are made for people who like search syntax. whereti is meant for
    everyone: type a word, see the file, open it.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>
  <p>
    whereti is written in Rust. It reads the file table of each drive directly, follows changes through the journal
    Windows already keeps, and reads documents in a separate, limited process. The whole design, with its measurements,
    is in the <a href={REPO}>repository</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is paid for</h2>
  <p>
    whereti is free, and will stay free in full. A <a href="/supporter/">supporter license</a>, a license key in your
    name or your company's, is there for people and companies who want to pay for the work. There are no ads and no data to sell, because whereti collects none.
  </p>

  <h2>If we stop</h2>
  <p>
    whereti needs no account and no server to search, so it keeps working exactly as it does today; the update check
    would simply find nothing new. The code is MIT licensed, so anyone can continue it.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. The company's details are in the <a href="/legal/">legal notice</a>.</p>
</Base>
